# Cheap-Model Routing Without Quality Regressions

"Save 70% on LLM inference by routing simple queries to small models!"

Every model gateway claims this. Almost none provide the mathematical evidence to prove that user experience hasn't quietly collapsed.

When routing simple queries to a cheaper model, the fundamental danger is **quality regression**: requests that look simple on the surface (e.g., tricky edge cases, subtle multi-step reasoning, or ambiguous questions) fail silently. If you only measure dollars saved, you are blind to user dissatisfaction until churn spikes.

---

## 1. The Verification Gap in Model Routers

Traditional model routers use heuristics, token counts, or a lightweight classifier. But once enabled, how do you verify that the cheap model produced an answer equivalent in quality to the expensive baseline?

Most teams cannot answer this without full double-billing (calling both models on every request) or conducting expensive offline human evals weeks later.

---

## 2. Shadow Sampling & Debiased Pairwise Judging

ProofGate introduces a continuous, in-flight quality verification loop:
1. **Continuous Shadow Sampling**: A configurable percentage (e.g. 5%) of smart-routed queries are shadowed to the strong model asynchronously.
2. **Position-Debiased Pairwise Judge**: The cheap and strong model outputs are evaluated head-to-head. To eliminate position bias (LLM judges overwhelmingly prefer the first answer presented), ProofGate swaps output orders ($A/B$ and $B/A$) and averages the score.
3. **Paired Bootstrap Confidence Intervals**: Rather than tracking a noisy arithmetic mean, ProofGate computes a 2,000-iteration paired bootstrap 95% confidence interval on the quality delta:

$$\Delta = \text{Score}_{\text{cheap}} - \text{Score}_{\text{strong}}$$

